Number,Question,Answer,Area
1,"A body of rock with distinctive characteristics that allow geologists to map, describe, and name it as a distinct unit.",Formation,Geology
2,"The branch of geology concerned with the study, description, and classification of layered rock sequences and their time relationships.",Stratigraphy,Geology
3,"Rock formed by the accumulation and lithification of sediment, such as sand, mud, or organic material.",Sedimentary rock,Geology
4,Rock formed from the cooling and solidification of molten magma or lava.,Igneous rock,Geology
5,"Rock formed by the transformation of pre-existing rock through heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids.",Metamorphic rock,Geology
6,"The physical character of a rock, including its mineral composition, texture, and color.",Lithology,Geology
7,A body of rock with specific characteristics that reflect the environment in which it was deposited.,Facies,Geology
8,"The physical, chemical, and biological setting in which sediment is deposited, such as a river, delta, or deep marine basin.",Depositional environment,Geology
9,"A surface representing a gap in the geologic record, where deposition was interrupted by erosion or non-deposition.",Unconformity,Geology
10,A fracture or zone of fractures in rock along which there has been significant displacement.,Fault,Geology
11,"A bend in rock layers caused by tectonic stress, forming structures such as anticlines and synclines.",Fold,Geology
12,"An upward-arched fold in rock layers, often forming a structural trap for hydrocarbons.",Anticline,Geology
13,"A downward-curved fold in rock layers, with the youngest strata found at its center.",Syncline,Geology
14,A geologic configuration that allows hydrocarbons to accumulate and be retained within a reservoir rock.,Trap (structural/stratigraphic),Geology
15,An impermeable rock layer that prevents the upward migration of hydrocarbons out of a reservoir.,Seal (caprock),Geology
16,A rock rich in organic matter capable of generating oil or gas when subjected to sufficient heat and pressure over time.,Source rock,Geology
17,"The movement of hydrocarbons from a source rock to a reservoir rock, or within a reservoir.",Migration,Geology
18,A large depression in the Earth's crust where sediments accumulate over geologic time.,Basin,Geology
19,"A regional-scale depression filled with sediment, often the site of hydrocarbon generation and accumulation.",Sedimentary basin,Geology
20,A single layer of sedimentary rock distinguishable from adjacent layers by composition or texture.,Stratum (bed),Geology
21,"An area where bedrock or sediment is exposed at the Earth's surface, without a cover of soil or vegetation.",Outcrop,Geology
22,"The boundary marking a time gap between two rock units, often indicating erosion or a depositional hiatus.",Unconformity surface,Geology
23,"The physical, chemical, and biological changes that sediment undergoes after deposition and before significant metamorphism.",Diagenesis,Geology
24,"The reduction in sediment volume due to the weight of overlying material, expelling pore fluids and reducing porosity.",Compaction,Geology
25,"The process by which minerals precipitate in the pore spaces of sediment, binding grains together into solid rock.",Cementation,Geology
26,A sedimentary rock composed of fragments (clasts) derived from the weathering and erosion of pre-existing rocks.,Clastic rock,Geology
27,"A sedimentary rock composed primarily of carbonate minerals, such as limestone or dolomite.",Carbonate rock,Geology
28,"A sedimentary rock composed mostly of calcium carbonate, often formed from marine organism remains.",Limestone,Geology
29,"A sedimentary rock or mineral composed of calcium magnesium carbonate, often formed through alteration of limestone.",Dolomite,Geology
30,"A fine-grained sedimentary rock formed from compacted mud, silt, and clay, often acting as a source rock or seal.",Shale,Geology
31,"A sedimentary rock composed of cemented sand-sized mineral or rock grains, commonly serving as a reservoir rock.",Sandstone,Geology
32,"A sedimentary rock composed of rounded, gravel-sized fragments cemented within a finer matrix.",Conglomerate,Geology
33,A naturally occurring inorganic solid with a defined chemical composition and crystal structure.,Mineral,Geology
34,"A common rock-forming mineral composed of silicon dioxide, known for its hardness and resistance to weathering.",Quartz,Geology
35,"A group of common rock-forming minerals composed of aluminum silicates, abundant in igneous and metamorphic rocks.",Feldspar,Geology
36,"A group of fine-grained minerals formed from the weathering of silicate rocks, common in shales and soils.",Clay mineral,Geology
37,The average diameter of the individual particles that make up a sediment or sedimentary rock.,Grain size,Geology
38,"The size, shape, and arrangement of the grains or crystals that make up a rock.",Texture (rock),Geology
39,The layered structure of sedimentary rock resulting from successive depositional events.,Bedding,Geology
40,"Inclined layering within a sedimentary bed, formed by the migration of ripples or dunes during deposition.",Cross-bedding,Geology
41,"Very thin layering in sedimentary rock, typically less than one centimeter thick.",Lamination,Geology
42,"The removal of rock or sediment material by natural agents such as water, wind, or ice.",Erosion,Geology
43,The physical and chemical breakdown of rocks and minerals at or near the Earth's surface.,Weathering,Geology
44,"The study of the large-scale processes that shape the structure of the Earth's crust, including plate movement and deformation.",Tectonics,Geology
45,The theory describing the movement of large rigid plates that make up the Earth's outer shell.,Plate tectonics,Geology
46,"The gradual sinking or downward movement of the Earth's surface, often associated with sediment loading or fluid extraction.",Subsidence,Geology
47,"The upward movement of the Earth's crust, often associated with tectonic activity.",Uplift,Geology
48,"A geologic structure formed when a mobile, less dense material (such as salt) rises and deforms overlying rock layers.",Diapir,Geology
49,"A dome-shaped structure formed by the upward flow of a salt body, often creating structural traps for hydrocarbons.",Salt dome,Geology
50,A sedimentary basin formed by the stretching and thinning of the Earth's crust along an extensional fault system.,Rift basin,Geology
51,A sedimentary basin formed adjacent to a mountain belt due to the flexural loading of the crust.,Foreland basin,Geology
52,"A tectonically stable continental margin not associated with a plate boundary, often characterized by thick sediment accumulation.",Passive margin,Geology
53,"A continental margin located at or near a plate boundary, typically associated with seismic and volcanic activity.",Active margin,Geology
54,A standardized chronological framework used to describe the timing and relationships of events in Earth's history.,Geologic time scale,Geology
55,"A major division of geologic time, subdivided into periods.",Era,Geology
56,"A unit of geologic time shorter than an era, used to subdivide Earth's history based on rock strata.",Period (geologic),Geology
57,"A subdivision of a geologic period, representing a shorter interval of geologic time.",Epoch,Geology
58,The branch of stratigraphy that uses fossil content to date and correlate rock layers.,Biostratigraphy,Geology
59,The preserved remains or traces of ancient organisms found within sedimentary rock.,Fossil,Geology
60,"A fossil used to identify and date the rock strata in which it is found, due to its widespread occurrence over a limited time interval.",Index fossil,Geology
61,The process of establishing the equivalence of rock units or time intervals across different locations.,Correlation (stratigraphic),Geology
62,A map showing lines of equal thickness of a given rock unit.,Isopach map,Geology
63,"A map depicting the elevation of a specific geologic surface, used to identify structural highs and traps.",Structure map,Geology
64,"A diagram representing a vertical slice through the Earth, showing the arrangement of rock layers and structures.",Cross section (geologic),Geology
65,The angle at which a rock layer or geologic surface is inclined from the horizontal.,Dip,Geology
66,The compass direction of the line formed by the intersection of a geologic surface with a horizontal plane.,Strike,Geology
67,"An unconformity where older, tilted or folded rock layers are overlain by younger, more horizontal layers.",Angular unconformity,Geology
68,"An unconformity where the rock layers above and below are parallel, but a time gap exists due to erosion or non-deposition.",Disconformity,Geology
69,An unconformity where sedimentary rock overlies older igneous or metamorphic rock.,Nonconformity,Geology
70,"A relative fall in sea level causing the shoreline to move seaward, often depositing coarser sediments over finer ones.",Regression (sea level),Geology
71,"A relative rise in sea level causing the shoreline to move landward, often depositing finer sediments over coarser ones.",Transgression (sea level),Geology
72,"A branch of stratigraphy that studies rock relationships within a chronological framework of repetitive, genetically related strata bounded by unconformities.",Sequence stratigraphy,Geology
73,"A sedimentary deposit formed by a turbidity current, typically found in deep marine environments.",Turbidite,Geology
74,"Sediment accumulated where a river enters a standing body of water, forming a delta.",Deltaic deposit,Geology
75,Sediment deposited by river processes.,Fluvial deposit,Geology
76,"Sediment deposited by wind action, typically well-sorted and fine-grained.",Aeolian deposit,Geology
77,"A sedimentary rock formed by the precipitation of minerals from evaporating water, such as salt or gypsum.",Evaporite,Geology
78,A hydrocarbon trap formed by changes in porosity or permeability resulting from diagenetic processes rather than structural deformation.,Diagenetic trap,Geology
79,"A hydrocarbon trap formed by changes in rock type, porosity, or permeability rather than structural deformation.",Stratigraphic trap,Geology
80,"A hydrocarbon trap formed by deformation of rock layers, such as folding or faulting.",Structural trap,Geology
81,"The older, typically crystalline igneous or metamorphic rock that underlies sedimentary sequences.",Basement rock,Geology
82,The rock or sediment layers lying above a specific formation or point of interest.,Overburden,Geology
83,The proportion of reservoir-quality rock relative to the total thickness of a given interval.,Net-to-gross ratio,Geology
84,Variability in rock properties such as porosity and permeability throughout a reservoir.,Heterogeneity (reservoir),Geology
85,"Changes in a rock's mineralogy, texture, or porosity resulting from post-depositional processes.",Diagenetic alteration,Geology
86,The origin or source area from which the sediment composing a rock was derived.,Provenance,Geology
87,An assemblage of related sedimentary environments and processes that together produce a characteristic body of sediment.,Depositional system,Geology
88,"The microscopic study and description of rocks, particularly their mineral composition and texture.",Petrography,Geology
89,A thinly sliced sample of rock mounted on a glass slide for examination under a microscope.,Thin section,Geology
90,"The study and reconstruction of the physical geography (land, sea, climate) of the Earth at a given time in the geologic past.",Paleogeography,Geology
91,The ancient environmental conditions under which a rock or sediment sequence was deposited.,Paleoenvironment,Geology
92,A graphical representation showing relative changes in sea level through geologic time.,Sea level curve,Geology
93,"A stratigraphic relationship in which younger strata progressively extend and terminate against an older, inclined surface.",Onlap,Geology
94,A stratigraphic relationship in which successive depositional units shift progressively basinward over time.,Offlap,Geology
95,"A fault that displays greater displacement in older strata than in younger ones, indicating movement occurred during deposition.",Growth fault,Geology
96,"A curved fault surface that becomes shallower in dip with depth, commonly associated with extensional tectonics.",Listric fault,Geology
97,An elevated block of crust bounded by normal faults on either side.,Horst,Geology
98,A depressed block of crust bounded by normal faults on either side.,Graben,Geology
99,"A landscape or subsurface feature formed by the dissolution of soluble rocks such as limestone, often creating cavities and irregular porosity.",Karst,Geology
100,Pore space in a rock created or modified after deposition through processes such as dissolution or dolomitization.,Diagenetic porosity,Geology
101,"The process of analyzing well log, core, and other subsurface data to determine the properties and hydrocarbon potential of a formation.",Formation evaluation,Formation Evaluation
102,"A recorded measurement of a physical property of a formation, plotted against depth, used to characterize the subsurface.",Well log,Formation Evaluation
103,A method of acquiring formation data using instruments lowered into the wellbore on an electrical cable.,Wireline logging,Formation Evaluation
104,A technique that acquires formation property data in real time while the well is being drilled.,Logging while drilling (LWD),Formation Evaluation
105,"A system that measures directional and drilling parameters in real time during drilling, often paired with LWD tools.",Measurement while drilling (MWD),Formation Evaluation
106,The fraction of a rock's total volume made up of void space capable of storing fluids.,Porosity,Formation Evaluation
107,"The interconnected pore volume in a rock that can contribute to fluid flow, excluding isolated pores.",Effective porosity,Formation Evaluation
108,"The total pore volume in a rock, including both connected and isolated pore spaces.",Total porosity,Formation Evaluation
109,A measure of a rock's ability to allow fluids to flow through its interconnected pore network.,Permeability,Formation Evaluation
110,"The fraction of pore space in a rock occupied by water, as opposed to hydrocarbons.",Water saturation (Sw),Formation Evaluation
111,The fraction of pore space in a rock occupied by oil or gas.,Hydrocarbon saturation,Formation Evaluation
112,The minimum water saturation remaining in a reservoir rock that cannot be further reduced by hydrocarbon displacement.,Irreducible water saturation,Formation Evaluation
113,"A log that measures the electrical resistivity of a formation, used to help distinguish hydrocarbons from water in the pore space.",Resistivity log,Formation Evaluation
114,"A log that measures the natural radioactivity of a formation, commonly used to distinguish shale from other lithologies.",Gamma ray log,Formation Evaluation
115,"A log that records naturally occurring electrical potential differences in the borehole, used to identify permeable zones and formation boundaries.",Spontaneous potential (SP) log,Formation Evaluation
116,"A log that measures the hydrogen content of a formation, used primarily to estimate porosity.",Neutron log,Formation Evaluation
117,"A log that measures the bulk density of a formation, used to estimate porosity and identify lithology.",Density log,Formation Evaluation
118,"A log that measures the travel time of sound waves through a formation, used to estimate porosity and rock mechanical properties.",Sonic log,Formation Evaluation
119,"A log that measures the diameter of the wellbore, used to detect washouts, mud cake, and other borehole irregularities.",Caliper log,Formation Evaluation
120,A thin layer of solids deposited on the wellbore wall by drilling fluid filtrate invading a permeable formation.,Mud cake,Formation Evaluation
121,The process by which the liquid component of drilling fluid penetrates a permeable formation near the wellbore.,Invasion (mud filtrate invasion),Formation Evaluation
122,The region near the wellbore where formation fluids have been largely displaced by mud filtrate.,Flushed zone,Formation Evaluation
123,The region of a formation affected by the invasion of drilling fluid filtrate.,Invaded zone,Formation Evaluation
124,The undisturbed part of a formation beyond the reach of drilling fluid invasion.,Virgin zone,Formation Evaluation
125,"A parameter relating the resistivity of a fully water-saturated rock to the resistivity of the water itself, related to porosity.",Formation factor,Formation Evaluation
126,"A widely used empirical formula relating water saturation to porosity, formation water resistivity, and formation resistivity.",Archie's equation,Formation Evaluation
127,"The thickness of reservoir rock that meets specified minimum criteria for porosity, permeability, and hydrocarbon saturation to be considered productive.",Net pay,Formation Evaluation
128,"The total thickness of a reservoir interval, including both productive and non-productive rock.",Gross pay,Formation Evaluation
129,The proportion of net pay relative to the gross thickness of a given interval.,Net-to-gross ratio,Formation Evaluation
130,A minimum or maximum threshold value applied to a petrophysical property to distinguish reservoir from non-reservoir rock.,Cutoff (petrophysical),Formation Evaluation
131,"The fraction of a rock volume occupied by shale, typically estimated from gamma ray or other logs.",Shale volume (Vsh),Formation Evaluation
132,"The process of determining rock type from log responses, core data, or cuttings.",Lithology identification,Formation Evaluation
133,Laboratory measurement of physical and petrophysical properties on a physical rock sample recovered from the well.,Core analysis,Formation Evaluation
134,"Standard laboratory measurements performed on core samples, typically including porosity, permeability, and fluid saturation.",Routine core analysis (RCAL),Formation Evaluation
135,"Advanced laboratory tests on core samples, such as relative permeability, capillary pressure, and wettability studies.",Special core analysis (SCAL),Formation Evaluation
136,"The pressure difference across the interface between two immiscible fluids in a porous medium, influencing fluid distribution.",Capillary pressure,Formation Evaluation
137,"The ratio of the effective permeability of a fluid phase to the absolute permeability of the rock, when more than one fluid is present.",Relative permeability,Formation Evaluation
138,The process of measuring pressure and collecting fluid samples directly from a formation using downhole tools.,Formation testing,Formation Evaluation
139,A wireline tool used to measure formation pressure and collect fluid samples at multiple depths in a well.,Repeat formation tester (RFT),Formation Evaluation
140,"A temporary completion test performed during drilling to evaluate the production potential, pressure, and fluid type of a formation.",Drill stem test (DST),Formation Evaluation
141,"The rate of change of pressure with depth within a fluid column, used to identify fluid contacts and fluid types.",Pressure gradient,Formation Evaluation
142,"The boundary depth separating two different fluids in a reservoir, such as the oil-water or gas-oil contact.",Fluid contact,Formation Evaluation
143,The depth in a reservoir marking the boundary between the gas cap and the underlying oil zone.,Gas-oil contact (GOC),Formation Evaluation
144,The depth in a reservoir marking the boundary between the oil zone and the underlying water zone.,Oil-water contact (OWC),Formation Evaluation
145,"The depth at which capillary pressure between water and hydrocarbon is theoretically zero, used as a reference for fluid contacts.",Free water level (FWL),Formation Evaluation
146,"The process of describing the geometry, properties, and heterogeneity of a reservoir using integrated geologic and petrophysical data.",Reservoir characterization,Formation Evaluation
147,The study of the physical and chemical properties of rocks and their interaction with fluids.,Petrophysics,Formation Evaluation
148,"The process of analyzing well log responses to determine formation properties such as lithology, porosity, and fluid content.",Log interpretation,Formation Evaluation
149,A graphical technique that plots two or more log measurements against each other to help identify lithology or fluid type.,Cross-plot (log analysis),Formation Evaluation
150,A cross-plot of resistivity versus porosity used to estimate water saturation and identify hydrocarbon-bearing zones.,Pickett plot,Formation Evaluation
151,The electrical resistivity of the water contained within a formation's pore space.,Formation water resistivity (Rw),Formation Evaluation
152,The electrical resistivity of the liquid portion of drilling fluid that invades a permeable formation.,Mud filtrate resistivity (Rmf),Formation Evaluation
153,"The actual, uninvaded resistivity of a formation, ideally reflecting its true fluid content.",True resistivity (Rt),Formation Evaluation
154,"The resistivity value measured by a logging tool, which may be affected by invasion, borehole effects, or adjacent beds.",Apparent resistivity,Formation Evaluation
155,The depth marking the transition between two distinct rock layers with differing properties.,Bed boundary,Formation Evaluation
156,The smallest bed thickness that a logging tool can accurately distinguish or measure.,Vertical resolution (logging),Formation Evaluation
157,"Adjustments applied to raw log measurements to account for effects such as borehole size, mud type, and temperature.",Borehole environment correction,Formation Evaluation
158,"A high-resolution log that provides a visual representation of the borehole wall, used to identify fractures, bedding, and structural features.",Formation image log,Formation Evaluation
159,A logging tool used to measure the dip and azimuth of formation bedding planes.,Dipmeter,Formation Evaluation
160,"A logging technique that measures the response of hydrogen nuclei in formation fluids, used to estimate porosity, pore size, and fluid type.",NMR logging (nuclear magnetic resonance),Formation Evaluation
161,A logging technique that measures the elemental composition of a formation to aid in lithology and mineralogy determination.,Elemental capture spectroscopy,Formation Evaluation
162,"The continuous monitoring and recording of drilling parameters, cuttings, and gas readings during drilling operations.",Mud logging,Formation Evaluation
163,"An indication of the presence of oil or gas in cuttings, cores, or mud, observed during drilling.",Show (hydrocarbon show),Formation Evaluation
164,The examination of rock fragments brought to surface during drilling to help determine lithology and hydrocarbon presence.,Cuttings analysis,Formation Evaluation
165,"A small rock sample taken directly from the wellbore wall using a specialized wireline tool, without removing the drill string.",Sidewall core,Formation Evaluation
166,A continuous cylindrical rock sample recovered from the wellbore using a coring bit and core barrel.,Whole core,Formation Evaluation
167,"A qualitative and quantitative assessment of a rock's ability to store and transmit fluids, based on porosity and permeability.",Reservoir quality,Formation Evaluation
168,"The variation of rock properties, such as porosity and permeability, throughout a reservoir.",Heterogeneity (reservoir),Formation Evaluation
169,"The variation of a rock property, such as permeability or resistivity, depending on the direction of measurement.",Anisotropy,Formation Evaluation
170,A measurement taken to determine the pore pressure of a formation at a specific depth.,Formation pressure test,Formation Evaluation
171,A condition in which formation pressure is significantly higher than the normal hydrostatic pressure expected at a given depth.,Overpressure,Formation Evaluation
172,Formation pressure that is consistent with the hydrostatic gradient expected from a column of formation water.,Normal pressure,Formation Evaluation
173,The rate at which temperature increases with depth within the Earth's subsurface.,Temperature gradient,Formation Evaluation
174,"The temperature measured at the bottom of the wellbore, often recorded during logging operations.",Bottom hole temperature (BHT),Formation Evaluation
175,A factor used to convert the volume of oil or gas at reservoir conditions to its equivalent volume at surface conditions.,Formation volume factor,Formation Evaluation
176,"The process of collecting a representative sample of reservoir fluid, typically using a downhole formation tester.",Fluid sampling,Formation Evaluation
177,"Laboratory analysis of the pressure-volume-temperature behavior of reservoir fluids, used to characterize fluid properties.",PVT analysis,Formation Evaluation
178,The tendency of a rock surface to preferentially be in contact with one fluid phase (such as water or oil) over another.,Wettability,Formation Evaluation
179,"A measure of the complexity of the flow path through a porous medium, relative to a straight-line path.",Tortuosity,Formation Evaluation
180,"The density of the solid mineral material composing a rock, excluding pore space.",Grain density,Formation Evaluation
181,"The overall density of a rock, including both the solid mineral framework and the fluids within its pore space.",Bulk density,Formation Evaluation
182,"A reference log value representing the typical response of shale, used as a comparison point in log interpretation.",Shale baseline,Formation Evaluation
183,A reference log value representing the typical response of a hydrocarbon- or water-bearing sand with minimal shale content.,Clean sand line,Formation Evaluation
184,"The angle of inclination of a rock layer relative to horizontal, as observed at a specific location.",Formation dip,Formation Evaluation
185,"A measurement of the wellbore's inclination and azimuth at various depths, used to determine its trajectory.",Deviation survey,Formation Evaluation
186,The process of adjusting log data from different tools or wells to a common reference scale for consistent comparison.,Log normalization,Formation Evaluation
187,"A rapid, approximate evaluation of formation properties using standard log cross-plots and rules of thumb.",Quick-look interpretation,Formation Evaluation
188,"A mathematical framework used to relate log measurements to formation properties such as porosity, saturation, and lithology.",Petrophysical model,Formation Evaluation
189,A petrophysical model that accounts for the effect of clay content when estimating porosity and water saturation in shaly reservoirs.,Shaly sand model,Formation Evaluation
190,A petrophysical model that distinguishes between clay-bound water and free formation water when calculating water saturation.,Dual water model,Formation Evaluation
191,"A high-resolution wireline tool that generates detailed images of the borehole wall to identify fractures, faults, and bedding features.",Formation micro-imager (FMI),Formation Evaluation
192,"The product of a rock's density and its acoustic velocity, used in seismic and log-based reservoir characterization.",Acoustic impedance,Formation Evaluation
193,"A type of sound wave in which particle motion is parallel to the direction of wave propagation, measured by sonic logging tools.",Compressional wave (P-wave),Formation Evaluation
194,"A type of sound wave in which particle motion is perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation, used to assess rock mechanical properties.",Shear wave (S-wave),Formation Evaluation
195,"Physical characteristics of a rock, such as elasticity and strength, that describe how it responds to stress.",Rock mechanical properties,Formation Evaluation
196,"A measure of a rock's stiffness, describing the relationship between stress and strain under uniaxial loading.",Young's modulus,Formation Evaluation
197,A measure of a rock's tendency to deform in directions perpendicular to an applied load.,Poisson's ratio,Formation Evaluation
198,"Physical characteristics describing how a rock deforms and recovers under applied stress, important for geomechanics.",Elastic properties,Formation Evaluation
199,"A reduction in the natural permeability of a formation near the wellbore caused by drilling, completion, or production processes.",Formation damage,Formation Evaluation
200,A dimensionless parameter representing additional pressure drop near the wellbore due to formation damage or stimulation.,Skin factor,Formation Evaluation
201,"The machine and structure used to drill a wellbore, including the derrick, drawworks, mud pumps, and power generation equipment.",Rig,Drilling
202,A large valve installed at the wellhead that can be closed to control pressure and prevent an uncontrolled flow of formation fluids to surface.,BOP (Blowout Preventer),Drilling
203,"A liquid or gas circulated through the well during drilling to cool the bit, carry cuttings to surface, and control formation pressure.",Drilling fluid (mud),Drilling
204,"The assembly of pipe, tools, and the bit connecting the surface equipment to the bottom of the hole, used to transmit rotation and weight.",Drill string,Drilling
205,The cutting tool at the bottom of the drill string responsible for crushing or shearing the rock formation.,Drill bit,Drilling
206,Large-diameter steel pipe run into an openhole and cemented in place to provide structural support and isolate formations.,Casing,Drilling
207,The process of pumping cement slurry into the annular space between the casing and the formation to secure the casing and isolate zones.,Cementing,Drilling
208,The surface equipment that supports the casing and production strings and provides control access to the well.,Wellhead,Drilling
209,"An unwanted influx of formation fluids (gas, oil, or water) into the wellbore because formation pressure exceeds the pressure exerted by the mud.",Kick,Drilling
210,"An uncontrolled flow of formation fluids to the surface, typically the result of an unmanaged kick.",Blowout,Drilling
211,"The density of the drilling fluid, used to control formation pressure while drilling.",Mud weight,Drilling
212,"The space between two concentric objects in the well, such as between the drill string and the wellbore wall, or between two casing strings.",Annulus,Drilling
213,The pressure exerted by fluids contained within the pore spaces of a rock formation.,Formation pressure,Drilling
214,The pressure of fluid held within the pore spaces of a rock.,Pore pressure,Drilling
215,"The pressure required to fracture a formation at a given depth, expressed per unit of depth.",Fracture gradient,Drilling
216,A drilling technique that intentionally controls the wellbore trajectory to reach a target other than straight down.,Directional drilling,Drilling
217,A well drilled so its final section is oriented at roughly 90 degrees from vertical within the reservoir.,Horizontal well,Drilling
218,The angle between the wellbore axis and the vertical at a given point.,Deviation,Drilling
219,"The hole drilled into the earth during exploration or production operations, whether cased or open.",Wellbore,Drilling
220,A section of the wellbore that has not been cased or cemented.,Openhole,Drilling
221,A polygonal-section pipe that transmits rotation from the rotary table to the drill string.,Kelly,Drilling
222,"A motorized system suspended in the derrick that rotates the drill string from the top, replacing the rotary table and kelly.",Top drive,Drilling
223,A drilling rig component that provides rotation to the drill string.,Rotary table,Drilling
224,The vertical structure that supports the weight of the drill string and allows pipe handling during operations.,Derrick,Drilling
225,"The hoisting system of the drilling rig that controls the vertical movement of the traveling block, string, and other equipment.",Drawworks,Drilling
226,A high-pressure pump that circulates drilling fluid from the mud tanks down through the drill string and back.,Mud pump,Drilling
227,Surface equipment that separates drill cuttings from the mud using vibrating screens.,Shale shaker,Drilling
228,Rock fragments generated by the cutting action of the bit during drilling.,Cuttings,Drilling
229,A technique that acquires formation property data in real time while drilling the well.,Logging while drilling (LWD),Drilling
230,"A system that measures directional and drilling parameters (inclination, azimuth) in real time while drilling.",Measurement while drilling (MWD),Drilling
231,A method of acquiring formation data using tools lowered into the well on an electrical cable.,Wireline logging,Drilling
232,The process of extracting a cylindrical rock sample (core) from a formation for laboratory analysis.,Coring,Drilling
233,The process of creating holes through the casing and cement to establish communication between the wellbore and the producing formation.,Perforating,Drilling
234,The set of operations performed after drilling to prepare a well for production.,Completion,Drilling
235,"An operation performed on an existing well to repair, maintain, or improve its production capability.",Workover,Drilling
236,"An operation aimed at recovering tools, pipe, or foreign objects that have become stuck or lost in the wellbore.",Fishing,Drilling
237,"A condition in which the drill string cannot move freely within the wellbore due to friction, formation collapse, or other factors.",Stuck pipe,Drilling
238,The rotational force applied to the drill string needed to turn the bit.,Torque,Drilling
239,The frictional resistance opposing the axial movement of the drill string within the wellbore.,Drag,Drilling
240,The axial force applied to the bit to facilitate rock cutting during drilling.,Weight on bit (WOB),Drilling
241,"The speed at which the bit advances through the formation, typically expressed in feet or meters per hour.",Rate of penetration (ROP),Drilling
242,Continuous monitoring of mud and cuttings characteristics during drilling to detect hydrocarbon shows.,Mud logging,Drilling
243,"The lower section of the drill string that includes the bit, downhole motors, stabilizers, and measurement tools.",Bottom hole assembly (BHA),Drilling
244,A thick-walled section of pipe located at the bottom of the string that provides weight to the bit.,Drill collar,Drilling
245,A tool incorporated in the drill string to keep the bit centered and help control wellbore trajectory.,Stabilizer,Drilling
246,A downhole tool that converts hydraulic energy from the drilling fluid into mechanical rotation for the bit.,Mud motor,Drilling
247,A downhole tool that allows directional control of the wellbore trajectory while continuously rotating the string.,Rotary steerable system (RSS),Drilling
248,"The operation of pulling or running the entire drill string in or out of the wellbore, usually to change the bit or other equipment.",Trip,Drilling
249,The act of lowering (in) or removing (out) the drill string from the wellbore.,Tripping in / tripping out,Drilling
250,"A device that restricts fluid flow to control wellbore pressure, especially used during well control operations.",Choke,Drilling
251,An assembly of valves and lines at surface used to control wellbore pressure during well control operations.,Choke manifold,Drilling
252,The set of techniques and procedures used to prevent or manage the uncontrolled flow of formation fluids.,Well control,Drilling
253,Higher-density mud used to regain control of a well that has experienced a kick.,Kill mud,Drilling
254,"The continuous process of pumping drilling fluid from surface, through the string, and back up the annulus.",Circulation,Drilling
255,The partial or total loss of drilling fluid into the formation instead of returning to surface.,Lost circulation,Drilling
256,The moment when drilling of a new well formally begins.,Spud,Drilling
257,The final depth reached by a well at the conclusion of drilling.,Total depth (TD),Drilling
258,"The drilling of a new wellbore trajectory from an existing point within a well, typically to bypass an obstruction or reach a new target.",Sidetrack,Drilling
259,A component located at the bottom end of the casing string that guides it into the hole and aids cementing.,Casing shoe,Drilling
260,A casing string that does not extend to surface but is instead hung from an earlier casing string.,Liner,Drilling
261,Devices installed near the bottom of the casing that prevent backflow of cement and fluid into the pipe during cementing.,Float equipment,Drilling
262,A device installed on casing to keep it centered in the wellbore and improve cement job quality.,Centralizer,Drilling
263,"A mixture of cement, water, and additives pumped into the well during cementing operations.",Cement slurry,Drilling
264,A remedial technique that forces cement through perforations or cracks to seal off unwanted zones.,Squeeze cementing,Drilling
265,The set of methods used to determine the physical properties and production potential of a formation.,Formation evaluation,Drilling
266,A porous and permeable rock formation containing accumulations of hydrocarbons.,Reservoir,Drilling
267,The ability of a rock to allow fluids to flow through its interconnected pore spaces.,Permeability,Drilling
268,The fraction of a rock's total volume that consists of void space capable of storing fluids.,Porosity,Drilling
269,A condition in which the mud's hydrostatic pressure is greater than the formation pressure.,Overbalance,Drilling
270,"A condition in which the mud's hydrostatic pressure is less than the formation pressure, potentially allowing fluids into the wellbore.",Underbalance,Drilling
271,A drilling technique that uses a closed system to precisely control annular pressure throughout the operation.,Managed pressure drilling (MPD),Drilling
272,A drilling method in which wellbore pressure is intentionally kept below formation pressure.,Underbalanced drilling (UBD),Drilling
273,A section of the well that has been cased and cemented.,Cased hole,Drilling
274,"The acceptable range of mud weights, bounded by the pore pressure gradient and the fracture gradient.",Drilling window,Drilling
275,The process of effectively removing drill cuttings from the wellbore through fluid circulation.,Hole cleaning,Drilling
276,Pressure changes in the wellbore caused by the upward (swab) or downward (surge) movement of the drill string.,Swab / surge,Drilling
277,Modeling that estimates torque and friction forces expected to act on the string before or during drilling of a directional well.,Torque and drag analysis,Drilling
278,The ability of the wellbore walls to remain intact without collapsing or fracturing during and after drilling.,Wellbore stability,Drilling
279,A condition in which the drill string becomes stuck against the wellbore wall due to a pressure differential between the mud and the formation.,Differential sticking,Drilling
280,Metal objects or other foreign material that fall into or become lodged in the wellbore.,Junk,Drilling
281,A wedge-shaped tool used to deflect the bit's path at the start of a sidetrack.,Whipstock,Drilling
282,The application of a mechanical impact using a tool called a jar to help free stuck pipe.,Jarring,Drilling
283,The operation of passing back through and enlarging the diameter of an already-drilled hole using a rotating tool.,Reaming,Drilling
284,The depth at which the trajectory of a directional well begins to be intentionally deviated.,Kick-off point (KOP),Drilling
285,"The rate at which the inclination angle of a directional well increases, expressed in degrees per unit of depth.",Build rate,Drilling
286,"A measure of the rate of change of wellbore direction and inclination per unit length, indicating how sharp a bend is.",Dogleg severity,Drilling
287,The horizontal direction of the wellbore trajectory measured in degrees relative to north.,Azimuth,Drilling
288,The angle between the wellbore axis and true vertical at a given point.,Inclination,Drilling
289,The vertical distance measured from surface to a given point in the wellbore.,True vertical depth (TVD),Drilling
290,"The total length of the wellbore path measured along its course, rather than in a straight vertical line.",Measured depth (MD),Drilling
291,The complete assembly of casing installed in a specific section of the wellbore.,Casing string,Drilling
292,"The first string of casing set in a well, protecting shallow aquifers and providing initial structural support.",Surface casing,Drilling
293,A casing string set between the surface casing and the production casing to isolate problem zones.,Intermediate casing,Drilling
294,The final casing string installed to allow controlled production of hydrocarbons.,Production casing,Drilling
295,"The first pipe installed in a well, intended to prevent the collapse of unconsolidated near-surface sediments.",Conductor casing,Drilling
296,"A surface reservoir used to store, treat, and condition the drilling fluid.",Mud tank,Drilling
297,The equipment and processes used to remove unwanted solids from the drilling fluid.,Solids control,Drilling
298,A pipe connecting surface drilling equipment (rig/platform) to a subsea wellhead in offshore operations.,Riser,Drilling
299,A wellhead installed on the seabed in offshore drilling operations.,Subsea wellhead,Drilling
300,A mobile offshore drilling platform that rests on the seabed using extendable legs.,Jack-up rig,Drilling
301,A geophysical method that uses generated sound waves and their reflections from subsurface layers to image the Earth's structure.,Seismic survey,Petrophysics
302,A returning seismic wave generated when energy encounters a boundary between rock layers with differing acoustic properties.,Seismic reflection,Petrophysics
303,A geophysical method that analyzes seismic waves bent (refracted) at boundaries between layers of differing velocity.,Seismic refraction,Petrophysics
304,"A seismic survey that images a single vertical plane, or line, through the subsurface.",2D seismic,Petrophysics
305,"A seismic survey that images a volume of the subsurface, providing spatial detail in three dimensions.",3D seismic,Petrophysics
306,"Repeated 3D seismic surveys acquired over time at the same location, used to monitor changes in a reservoir during production.",4D seismic (time-lapse seismic),Petrophysics
307,"A device, such as a vibrator truck or air gun, used to generate seismic energy for a survey.",Seismic source,Petrophysics
308,"A land seismic source that generates a controlled, swept-frequency vibration transmitted into the ground through a truck-mounted plate.",Vibroseis,Petrophysics
309,A marine seismic source that releases compressed air into water to generate a seismic pulse.,Air gun,Petrophysics
310,A sensor used on land to detect and record ground motion caused by seismic waves.,Geophone,Petrophysics
311,A sensor used in marine environments to detect pressure changes caused by seismic waves in water.,Hydrophone,Petrophysics
312,"A cable towed behind a vessel containing hydrophones, used to record seismic data in marine surveys.",Streamer,Petrophysics
313,"A seismic recording instrument placed on the seafloor to capture seismic data, often used where streamers are impractical.",Ocean bottom seismometer (OBS),Petrophysics
314,The sequence of computational steps applied to raw seismic data to produce an interpretable image of the subsurface.,Seismic processing,Petrophysics
315,"A seismic processing step that repositions reflected energy to its correct subsurface location, improving image accuracy.",Migration (seismic),Petrophysics
316,A seismic processing step that combines multiple traces to improve the signal-to-noise ratio of the final image.,Stacking (seismic),Petrophysics
317,A seismic processing technique used to improve resolution by removing the effects of the source wavelet from recorded data.,Deconvolution,Petrophysics
318,"The variation in seismic travel time with source-receiver offset for a reflection from a horizontal layer, corrected during processing.",Normal moveout (NMO),Petrophysics
319,"A subsurface reflection point shared by multiple source-receiver pairs, used to improve data quality through stacking.",Common depth point (CDP),Petrophysics
320,"A single recording of ground or pressure motion over time at one receiver location, forming the basic unit of seismic data.",Seismic trace,Petrophysics
321,"The magnitude of a seismic wave, often used to infer rock and fluid properties within a formation.",Amplitude (seismic),Petrophysics
322,A seismic analysis technique that studies changes in reflection amplitude with source-receiver distance to help identify fluid content.,Amplitude versus offset (AVO),Petrophysics
323,"A quantitative measure derived from seismic data, used to highlight specific geologic or reservoir characteristics.",Seismic attribute,Petrophysics
324,"A technique that transforms seismic reflection data into quantitative rock property estimates, such as acoustic impedance.",Seismic inversion,Petrophysics
325,"The product of rock density and seismic velocity, used to characterize subsurface layers from seismic data.",Acoustic impedance,Petrophysics
326,The speed at which a seismic wave travels through a given rock layer.,Seismic velocity,Petrophysics
327,"The seismic velocity of a specific layer between two boundaries, as opposed to an average velocity from the surface.",Interval velocity,Petrophysics
328,"The mean seismic velocity of all layers above a given depth, measured from the surface.",Average velocity,Petrophysics
329,The process of converting seismic data recorded in travel time into estimated subsurface depth using velocity information.,Time-depth conversion,Petrophysics
330,"A representation of how seismic velocity varies throughout the subsurface, used for time-depth conversion and imaging.",Velocity model,Petrophysics
331,The total time for a seismic wave to travel from the source down to a reflector and back up to a receiver.,Two-way travel time (TWT),Petrophysics
332,The ability of seismic data to distinguish between closely spaced geologic features.,Seismic resolution,Petrophysics
333,A seismic survey in which sources are placed at the surface and receivers are positioned within a wellbore to calibrate and enhance surface seismic data.,Vertical seismic profile (VSP),Petrophysics
334,"A well survey that records the travel time of seismic waves from a surface source to receivers at known depths in a wellbore, used to calibrate seismic data.",Checkshot survey,Petrophysics
335,"A modeled seismic trace generated from well log data, used to tie well information to seismic data.",Synthetic seismogram,Petrophysics
336,The correlation of well log or geologic data with seismic data to calibrate depth and improve interpretation.,Well tie,Petrophysics
337,A mapped surface within a seismic volume representing a specific geologic boundary or reflector.,Horizon (seismic),Petrophysics
338,"The process of analyzing processed seismic data to identify geologic structures, stratigraphy, and potential hydrocarbon indicators.",Seismic interpretation,Petrophysics
339,A high-amplitude seismic anomaly that can indicate the presence of gas within a reservoir.,Bright spot,Petrophysics
340,A low-amplitude seismic anomaly that can indicate a change in fluid content or lithology within a reservoir.,Dim spot,Petrophysics
341,"A flat, horizontal seismic reflection often associated with a fluid contact, such as a gas-oil or oil-water boundary.",Flat spot,Petrophysics
342,"A seismic anomaly, such as a bright spot or flat spot, that suggests the possible presence of hydrocarbons.",Direct hydrocarbon indicator (DHI),Petrophysics
343,A geophysical method that measures variations in the Earth's gravitational field to infer subsurface density differences.,Gravity survey,Petrophysics
344,A geophysical method that measures variations in the Earth's magnetic field to infer subsurface geologic features.,Magnetic survey,Petrophysics
345,An instrument used to measure small variations in the Earth's gravitational field.,Gravimeter,Petrophysics
346,An instrument used to measure variations in the Earth's magnetic field.,Magnetometer,Petrophysics
347,A geophysical method that uses electromagnetic fields to detect variations in subsurface electrical resistivity.,Electromagnetic survey (EM),Petrophysics
348,"A marine electromagnetic survey technique that uses a towed source to detect resistive bodies, such as hydrocarbon reservoirs, beneath the seafloor.",Controlled-source electromagnetics (CSEM),Petrophysics
349,A geophysical method that uses natural variations in the Earth's electric and magnetic fields to investigate subsurface resistivity.,Magnetotellurics (MT),Petrophysics
350,"A deviation from the expected gravitational field value, indicating a subsurface density contrast.",Gravity anomaly,Petrophysics
351,A gravity anomaly value corrected for the effects of elevation and the mass of rock beneath a survey station.,Bouguer anomaly,Petrophysics
352,"The state of gravitational equilibrium between the Earth's crust and mantle, influencing regional gravity measurements.",Isostasy,Petrophysics
353,"The characteristic waveform generated by a seismic source, whose shape influences the resolution and character of the recorded data.",Wavelet (seismic),Petrophysics
354,A measure comparing the strength of a desired seismic signal to the level of background noise in the data.,Signal-to-noise ratio,Petrophysics
355,An unwanted seismic reflection that has bounced more than once between subsurface layers or the surface before being recorded.,Multiple (seismic),Petrophysics
356,An unwanted seismic reflection caused by energy traveling upward from the source or downward from the receiver before being recorded.,Ghost reflection,Petrophysics
357,An adjustment applied to seismic data to compensate for near-surface elevation and velocity variations.,Static correction,Petrophysics
358,An adjustment applied to seismic data to correct for travel time variations related to source-receiver offset.,Dynamic correction,Petrophysics
359,The horizontal distance between a seismic source and a receiver.,Offset (seismic),Petrophysics
360,"The number of individual traces combined during stacking to produce a single output trace, related to data redundancy.",Fold (seismic),Petrophysics
361,A small subsurface area used to group and organize seismic traces during 3D data processing.,Bin (seismic),Petrophysics
362,"The range of frequencies present in a seismic signal, influencing the resolution of subsurface imaging.",Seismic bandwidth,Petrophysics
363,"A seismic wavelet shape that is symmetric around a central peak, generally preferred for accurate interpretation.",Zero-phase wavelet,Petrophysics
364,A seismic wavelet shape in which most of the energy is concentrated near the beginning of the waveform.,Minimum-phase wavelet,Petrophysics
365,"Seismic data that has not yet been combined (stacked) across multiple source-receiver pairs, retaining offset information.",Prestack data,Petrophysics
366,"Seismic data that has already been combined (stacked) into a single trace per location, simplifying the volume for interpretation.",Poststack data,Petrophysics
367,"A seismic imaging technique that repositions reflectors using a velocity model, producing results in the time domain.",Time migration,Petrophysics
368,"A seismic imaging technique that repositions reflectors using a velocity model, producing results directly in depth.",Depth migration,Petrophysics
369,"An advanced seismic imaging technique applied before stacking that positions reflectors in true depth, often used in structurally complex areas.",Pre-stack depth migration (PSDM),Petrophysics
370,The variation of seismic velocity with the direction of wave propagation within a rock or rock sequence.,Anisotropy (seismic),Petrophysics
371,A measure of how quickly seismic wave energy is absorbed or attenuated as it travels through rock.,Q factor (seismic attenuation),Petrophysics
372,A seismic analysis technique that separates data into different frequency components to reveal subtle stratigraphic or structural features.,Spectral decomposition,Petrophysics
373,"A seismic attribute that measures the similarity of waveforms between adjacent traces, commonly used to highlight faults and discontinuities.",Coherence attribute,Petrophysics
374,"A seismic attribute that measures the bending of a mapped horizon, used to identify subtle structural features such as fractures.",Curvature attribute,Petrophysics
375,A seismic inversion method that estimates acoustic or elastic impedance from seismic reflection data.,Impedance inversion,Petrophysics
376,"A seismic inversion technique that estimates multiple rock properties, such as P-wave and S-wave impedance, from prestack seismic data.",Elastic inversion,Petrophysics
377,The study of the relationships between the physical properties of rocks and the seismic or geophysical measurements used to detect them.,Rock physics,Petrophysics
378,"A quantitative framework linking rock and fluid properties, such as porosity and saturation, to seismic velocity and density.",Rock physics model,Petrophysics
379,A rock physics equation relating the elastic properties of a rock to changes in its pore fluid content.,Gassmann's equation,Petrophysics
380,"An advanced seismic processing technique that iteratively updates a velocity model by matching the complete recorded waveform, rather than only travel times.",Full waveform inversion (FWI),Petrophysics
381,"A geophysical technique that uses background seismic noise, rather than an active source, to image or monitor the subsurface.",Ambient noise seismology,Petrophysics
382,"The detection and analysis of small seismic events, often used to monitor hydraulic fracturing or reservoir activity.",Microseismic monitoring,Petrophysics
383,"A geophysical method that records naturally occurring or induced seismic energy without using an active, controlled source.",Passive seismic,Petrophysics
384,The process of recording seismic data on land using sources and receivers deployed across the survey area.,Land seismic acquisition,Petrophysics
385,The process of recording seismic data at sea using a vessel-towed source and streamer or ocean-bottom receivers.,Marine seismic acquisition,Petrophysics
386,A standalone seismic receiver placed directly on the seafloor to record data independent of a towed streamer.,Ocean bottom node (OBN),Petrophysics
387,"A seismic acquisition design that records data over a broad range of source-receiver directions, improving subsurface imaging in complex areas.",Wide-azimuth survey,Petrophysics
388,"A seismic acquisition design that records data over a limited range of source-receiver directions, typical of conventional towed-streamer surveys.",Narrow-azimuth survey,Petrophysics
389,The planning process that determines source and receiver geometry to achieve desired subsurface imaging objectives.,Survey design (seismic),Petrophysics
390,"A survey that precisely measures the shape, size, and gravitational field of the Earth, often supporting geophysical interpretation.",Geodetic survey,Petrophysics
391,A magnetic survey conducted using instruments mounted on an aircraft to measure variations in the Earth's magnetic field over large areas.,Aeromagnetic survey,Petrophysics
392,"A geophysical technique that measures spatial variations in the rate of change of the gravitational field, offering higher-resolution density information.",Gravity gradiometry,Petrophysics
393,"A rock property estimated from seismic velocities, describing how a rock deforms perpendicular to an applied stress, useful in fluid and lithology discrimination.",Poisson's ratio (from seismic),Petrophysics
394,"The ratio of compressional wave velocity to shear wave velocity in a rock, commonly used to help distinguish lithology and fluid content.",Vp/Vs ratio,Petrophysics
395,The complete spatial and temporal distribution of seismic wave energy as it propagates through the subsurface.,Wavefield,Petrophysics
396,"The scattering of seismic energy from a small subsurface feature, such as a fault edge or pinch-out, producing a distinctive wave pattern.",Diffraction (seismic),Petrophysics
397,A modeling technique that simulates the path of seismic energy through a velocity model to predict travel times.,Ray tracing,Petrophysics
398,A technique that reconstructs a subsurface velocity model using travel times recorded from many source-receiver combinations.,Tomography (seismic),Petrophysics
399,"Specialized software used by geophysicists to visualize, analyze, and interpret seismic and well data.",Interpretation workstation,Petrophysics
400,A measure of how well a synthetic seismogram matches the actual recorded seismic data at a well location.,Seismic-to-well tie quality,Petrophysics
401,"A controlled operation in which a well is produced under monitored conditions to gather data on flow rate, pressure, and fluid properties.",Well test,Production
402,A test conducted to determine a well's production capacity and the characteristics of the fluids it produces.,Production test,Production
403,"A prolonged production test, often lasting weeks or months, conducted to gather long-term reservoir performance and fluid data ahead of full field development.",Extended well test (EWT),Production
404,"A pressure transient test in which a well is shut in after a period of production, and the resulting pressure rise is recorded over time.",Buildup test,Production
405,A pressure transient test in which a well is placed on production at a constant rate while the resulting pressure decline is recorded.,Drawdown test,Production
406,A test in which fluid is injected into a well to evaluate formation properties such as permeability and injectivity.,Injection test,Production
407,A pressure transient test in which an injection well is shut in and the resulting pressure decline is recorded.,Falloff test,Production
408,A multi-well test in which one well is produced or injected while pressure response is monitored in one or more offset wells.,Interference test,Production
409,A type of interference test in which short pulses of production or injection are used to determine reservoir connectivity between wells.,Pulse test,Production
410,The interpretation of pressure changes over time during a well test to estimate reservoir and well properties.,Pressure transient analysis (PTA),Production
411,"A test used to determine the relationship between a well's flow rate and its flowing bottomhole pressure, especially in gas wells.",Deliverability test,Production
412,A well test conducted at several different flow rates to characterize well performance and reservoir behavior.,Multi-rate test,Production
413,"A gas well deliverability test in which the well is produced at several rates for equal time periods, with shut-in periods between each.",Isochronal test,Production
414,"A variation of the isochronal test in which shut-in periods are shortened, making the test faster to conduct while preserving key data.",Modified isochronal test,Production
415,A gas well deliverability test conducted at successively increasing flow rates without intervening shut-in periods.,Flow after flow test,Production
416,The theoretical maximum rate at which a gas well could flow against atmospheric pressure at the wellhead.,Absolute open flow potential (AOF),Production
417,"A treatment applied to a well, such as acidizing or fracturing, intended to improve its productivity by reducing near-wellbore restrictions or enhancing permeability.",Stimulation (well),Production
418,"The specific goals defined before a well test, such as estimating permeability, skin, reservoir pressure, or boundary distance.",Well testing objectives,Production
419,The process of assembling and connecting surface and downhole equipment at the wellsite in preparation for a well test.,Test rig-up,Production
420,"A well testing effect caused by fluid expansion or compression within the wellbore itself, which can mask early-time reservoir response.",Wellbore storage,Production
421,The approximate distance into the reservoir that a pressure transient has affected at a given time during a well test.,Radius of investigation,Production
422,The pressure measured at the bottom of a wellbore while the well is actively producing.,Flowing bottomhole pressure (FBHP),Production
423,The pressure measured at the bottom of a wellbore after it has been shut in long enough to stabilize.,Static bottomhole pressure (SBHP),Production
424,The pressure recorded at the wellhead or bottomhole after a well has been closed off from production or injection.,Shut-in pressure,Production
425,"The pressure measured at the surface equipment of a well, either while flowing or shut in.",Wellhead pressure,Production
426,"An instrument placed downhole to record pressure, and often temperature, during a well test.",Bottomhole pressure gauge,Production
427,A tool run into a well to continuously measure and log pressure data at depth during a test.,Downhole pressure recorder,Production
428,A downhole pressure and temperature recorder that stores data internally for retrieval after the tool is removed from the well.,Memory gauge,Production
429,A downhole pressure and temperature gauge that transmits real-time data to surface during a well test.,Surface readout (SRO) gauge,Production
430,"Surface equipment used during a well test to separate produced fluids into oil, gas, and water phases for individual measurement.",Test separator,Production
431,"A device that measures the flow rates of oil, gas, and water simultaneously without requiring separation of the phases.",Multiphase flow meter,Production
432,The diameter of the restriction used at surface or downhole to control the flow rate of a well during testing or production.,Choke size,Production
433,"A measure of a well's ability to produce fluid, expressed as the flow rate per unit of pressure drawdown.",Productivity index (PI),Production
434,A curve describing the relationship between a well's flowing bottomhole pressure and its corresponding production rate.,Inflow performance relationship (IPR),Production
435,"A curve describing the relationship between wellhead pressure and flow rate, accounting for pressure losses as fluid travels up the tubing.",Vertical lift performance (VLP),Production
436,A method that combines inflow and outflow performance relationships at a common point in the well system to predict production rates.,Nodal analysis,Production
437,The study of pressure and flow behavior of fluids as they move through the tubing and wellbore during production.,Wellbore hydraulics,Production
438,The interpretation of well performance data collected at multiple flow rates to establish a deliverability relationship.,Multi-rate deliverability analysis,Production
439,A well test interpretation technique that compares measured pressure or rate data to a family of standard reservoir response curves.,Type curve matching,Production
440,"A graph with both axes on logarithmic scales, commonly used in pressure transient analysis to identify flow regimes.",Log-log plot,Production
441,A specialized semi-log plot used to analyze buildup test data and estimate reservoir pressure and permeability.,Horner plot,Production
442,A well test interpretation method that plots pressure against the logarithm of time to identify straight-line flow trends.,Semi-log analysis,Production
443,"A diagnostic plot of the rate of change of pressure with respect to the logarithm of time, used to identify flow regimes during a well test.",Derivative plot,Production
444,"A characteristic pattern of fluid movement toward the wellbore during a specific period of a well test, such as radial or linear flow.",Flow regime,Production
445,A flow regime in which fluid moves toward the wellbore from all directions within a cylindrical pattern.,Radial flow,Production
446,"A flow regime in which fluid moves toward the wellbore along parallel flow paths, often observed in fractured or channel reservoirs.",Linear flow,Production
447,A change in pressure behavior observed during a well test caused by the pressure transient reaching a reservoir boundary or barrier.,Boundary effect,Production
448,"A reservoir limit, such as a sealing fault, across which fluid cannot flow, identified through its effect on pressure transient data.",No-flow boundary,Production
449,"A reservoir limit, such as an aquifer or gas cap, that maintains pressure support and influences pressure transient behavior.",Constant-pressure boundary,Production
450,An extended well test designed to estimate the drainage area or connected volume of a reservoir.,Reservoir limit test,Production
451,"The representative pressure across a reservoir or drainage area, often estimated from extended buildup test data.",Average reservoir pressure,Production
452,"A theoretical pressure value obtained by extending the straight-line portion of a buildup plot, used to estimate reservoir pressure.",Extrapolated pressure (P*),Production
453,"A parameter combining reservoir permeability and net pay thickness, commonly derived from well test analysis.",Permeability-thickness product (kh),Production
454,"A reservoir property combining permeability, thickness, and fluid viscosity, describing the ease with which fluid can flow through the formation.",Transmissibility,Production
455,"The initial flow period after a well is opened, during which drilling or completion fluids and debris are removed from the near-wellbore area.",Wellbore cleanup,Production
456,The flow period conducted following well completion or stimulation to remove residual fluids before formal testing begins.,Cleanup flow,Production
457,"The controlled return of injected fluids, such as fracturing fluid, to surface following a stimulation treatment.",Flowback,Production
458,"A single-strand wire used to run and retrieve small tools in a wellbore, commonly used for gauge deployment during testing.",Slickline,Production
459,"A continuous length of flexible steel tubing spooled on a reel, used to convey tools and fluids into a wellbore for testing or intervention.",Coiled tubing,Production
460,"A tool placed near the reservoir that allows a well to be shut in at depth rather than at surface, reducing wellbore storage effects.",Downhole shut-in tool,Production
461,The assembly of tubing and downhole tools run into a well specifically to conduct a well test.,Test string,Production
462,A downhole device used to seal the annulus and isolate a specific zone of interest during a well test.,Packer (testing),Production
463,A valve installed in the test string that allows the well to be closed off at depth during shut-in periods of a test.,Downhole shut-in valve (DHSV),Production
464,"Wellhead equipment used during a well test to control flow, monitor pressure, and provide a means of well control at surface.",Surface test tree,Production
465,The assembly of valves and fittings installed at the wellhead to control the flow of fluids from a producing well.,Christmas tree,Production
466,The set of procedures and equipment used to maintain control of pressure and flow during testing operations.,Well control (testing operations),Production
467,"The controlled combustion of produced gas at surface, often used during well testing when no pipeline or storage is available.",Flaring,Production
468,A surface structure used to safely direct and combust produced gas during flow testing operations.,Burner boom,Production
469,"The process of collecting representative samples of produced oil, gas, or water during a well test for laboratory analysis.",Sampling (fluid),Production
470,"A fluid sample collected directly from downhole conditions, preserving original reservoir pressure and temperature characteristics.",Bottomhole sample,Production
471,"A fluid sample collected at surface equipment during a well test, typically at separator or wellhead conditions.",Surface sample,Production
472,"The ratio of the volume of gas produced to the volume of oil produced, measured at surface conditions.",Gas-oil ratio (GOR),Production
473,The percentage of water present in the total liquid volume produced by a well.,Water cut,Production
474,"The volume of liquid hydrocarbons recovered per unit volume of gas produced, typical of gas condensate wells.",Condensate yield,Production
475,A test used to characterize how flow rate changes with different choke sizes under a given set of well conditions.,Choke performance test,Production
476,A flow condition through a choke in which the flow rate becomes independent of downstream pressure.,Critical flow,Production
477,A staged process of gradually increasing choke size to bring a well to a target flow rate while managing pressure and sand production.,Bean-up procedure,Production
478,The process of detecting and measuring the amount of formation sand produced along with reservoir fluids during a test.,Sand production monitoring,Production
479,The flow velocity above which excessive erosion of tubing or surface equipment may occur due to produced sand or high rates.,Erosional velocity,Production
480,"The planning process used to define objectives, duration, rates, and equipment required for a specific well test.",Well test design,Production
481,"The total length of time allotted for a well test, including flow and shut-in periods, to achieve reliable data.",Test duration,Production
482,A mathematical principle used to account for the effects of multiple rate changes when analyzing pressure transient data.,Superposition (well testing),Production
483,"A technique used in well test analysis to adjust pressure data for varying flow rates, allowing comparison across different periods.",Rate normalization,Production
484,An analytical technique that converts variable-rate pressure data into an equivalent constant-rate response for interpretation.,Deconvolution (well testing),Production
485,"Pressure or rate data recorded shortly after a rate change in a well test, often influenced by wellbore storage effects.",Early-time data,Production
486,"Pressure or rate data recorded during the period of a well test that reflects the dominant reservoir flow regime, such as radial flow.",Middle-time data,Production
487,"Pressure or rate data recorded near the end of a well test, often influenced by reservoir boundaries or interference effects.",Late-time data,Production
488,Specialized software used to analyze pressure and rate data from a well test and estimate reservoir and well parameters.,Test interpretation software,Production
489,A numerical representation of a well and reservoir system used to match observed test data and validate interpreted parameters.,Simulation model (well test),Production
490,The process of adjusting model parameters until simulated pressure or rate data matches observed well test measurements.,History matching (well test),Production
491,"The distance from the wellbore to the tip of an induced hydraulic fracture, often estimated from well test analysis.",Fracture half-length,Production
492,"A measure of the ability of a hydraulic fracture to conduct fluid, combining fracture permeability and width.",Fracture conductivity,Production
493,"A reservoir model that represents fluid flow through two distinct systems, such as rock matrix and natural fractures.",Dual porosity model,Production
494,"A well test interpretation model that accounts for changes in reservoir properties, such as permeability, across a defined boundary.",Composite reservoir model,Production
495,A well test designed to evaluate the individual contribution and properties of multiple productive layers within a single well.,Layered reservoir testing,Production
496,"A downhole tool run to measure flow profile, fluid composition, and other parameters along the length of a producing wellbore.",Production logging tool (PLT),Production
497,A production logging measurement using a rotating device to determine fluid velocity within the wellbore at different depths.,Spinner survey,Production
498,A downhole temperature measurement used to identify producing intervals and detect anomalies such as crossflow or leaks.,Temperature survey (production),Production
499,"A measurement of how injected fluid is distributed along the length of a wellbore, typically obtained via production logging.",Injection profile,Production
500,"The unintended movement of fluid between different zones within a wellbore, often detected using production logging tools.",Crossflow,Production
